[38;2;124;124;124mWe have a sequence of positive integers a₁, a₂, a₃, ... and a positive integer [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mN. For each n > N, aₙ equals the number of times aₙ₋₁ appears in the list a₁, [0m
[38;2;124;124;124ma₂, ..., aₙ₋₁.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mWe need to prove that at least one of the odd-indexed subsequence (a₁, a₃, [0m
[38;2;124;124;124ma₅, ...) and the even-indexed subsequence (a₂, a₄, a₆, ...) is eventually [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mperiodic.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me think about this. Let me define the "count function": for n > N, aₙ = |{k[0m[38;2;124;124;124m< n : aₖ = aₙ₋₁}|.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me denote cₙ = aₙ. For n > N, cₙ = count of cₙ₋₁ in c₁, ..., cₙ₋₁.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me think about what happens. Let's define for each value v, the count of how[0m[38;2;124;124;124mmany times v has appeared so far. Let fₙ(v) = |{k ≤ n : aₖ = v}|.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThen for n > N: aₙ = fₙ₋₁(aₙ₋₁).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo aₙ₊₁ = fₙ(aₙ) = fₙ₋₁(aₙ) + [aₙ = aₙ] = fₙ₋₁(aₙ) + 1.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mWait, that's not quite right. fₙ(aₙ) = fₙ₋₁(aₙ) + 1 since aₙ is counted at [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mposition n.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo aₙ₊₁ = fₙ(aₙ) = fₙ₋₁(aₙ) + 1.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mHmm, but fₙ₋₁(aₙ) is the count of aₙ in positions 1..n-1, which is aₙ₊₁ - 1... [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mwait no.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me re-index. For n > N: aₙ = fₙ₋₁(aₙ₋₁).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mFor n+1 > N (i.e., n ≥ N): aₙ₊₁ = fₙ(aₙ).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mNow fₙ(aₙ) = fₙ₋₁(aₙ) + 1 (the +1 is because aₙ itself is at position n).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo aₙ₊₁ = fₙ₋₁(aₙ) + 1.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThis is a useful relation: for n ≥ N, aₙ₊₁ = fₙ₋₁(aₙ) + 1.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me think about this differently. Let's track the "frequency" of each value.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mKey observation: Let's think about what values can appear. If aₙ₋₁ = v, then aₙ [0m[38;2;124;124;124m= (number of times v appeared in a₁..aₙ₋₁).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me think about small examples to build intuition.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mExample: Start with a₁ = 1, N = 1.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- a₂ = count of 1 in {a₁} = 1.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- a₃ = count of 1 in {a₁, a₂} = {1, 1} = 2.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- a₄ = count of 2 in {1, 1, 2} = 1.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- a₅ = count of 1 in {1, 1, 2, 1} = 3.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- a₆ = count of 3 in {1, 1, 2, 1, 3} = 1.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- a₇ = count of 1 in {1, 1, 2, 1, 3, 1} = 4.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- a₈ = count of 4 in {1, 1, 2, 1, 3, 1, 4} = 1.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- a₉ = count of 1 in {1, 1, 2, 1, 3, 1, 4, 1} = 5.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo the pattern is: 1, 1, 2, 1, 3, 1, 4, 1, 5, 1, 6, 1, ...[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mOdd positions: a₁=1, a₃=2, a₅=3, a₇=4, a₉=5, ... → 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, ... (not [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mperiodic, goes to infinity)[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mEven positions: a₂=1, a₄=1, a₆=1, a₈=1, ... → 1, 1, 1, 1, ... (eventually [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mconstant, hence periodic)[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo in this case the even subsequence is eventually periodic (constant 1).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me think about the general structure. The relation aₙ₊₁ = fₙ₋₁(aₙ) + 1 for n[0m[38;2;124;124;124m≥ N is key.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me define gₙ(v) = fₙ(v) = number of times v appears in a₁, ..., aₙ.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThen for n ≥ N: aₙ₊₁ = gₙ₋₁(aₙ) + 1.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mAlso gₙ(v) = gₙ₋₁(v) + [aₙ = v].[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo gₙ is a non-decreasing function of n for each v.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me think about the two-step recurrence. For n ≥ N+1 (so that both n and n+1 [0m[38;2;124;124;124mare ≥ N):[0m

[38;2;124;124;124maₙ₊₂ = gₙ(aₙ₊₁) + 1 = gₙ₋₁(aₙ₊₁) + [aₙ = aₙ₊₁] + 1.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mAnd aₙ₊₁ = gₙ₋₁(aₙ) + 1.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mHmm, this is getting complex. Let me think about it from a higher level.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me consider the pair (aₙ, aₙ₊₁) for n ≥ N. We have:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- aₙ₊₁ = gₙ₋₁(aₙ) + 1[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- aₙ₊₂ = gₙ(aₙ₊₁) + 1 = gₙ₋₁(aₙ₊₁) + [aₙ = aₙ₊₁] + 1[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe state of the system at step n is essentially the entire frequency function [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mgₙ₋₁, which is high-dimensional. But maybe we can find a simpler description.[0m

